Sign in and store your snouty configuration

Sign in to the tenant, then save the tenant, its container registry and your
credential in the global snouty settings file, optionally under a named profile.
Login checks the credential with the tenant first, and saves nothing when the
tenant rejects it. Information not provided via args is asked for at the
terminal. Without a terminal, login uses the credential from the environment,
such as ANTITHESIS_API_KEY.

Login saves the registry that the tenant reports as your repository. Use
--repository to push to a different repository. When the registry does not
accept your snouty credential, sign in to it with `docker login` or
`podman login`.

NOTE: `snouty login` will offer to reuse your existing configuration values,
including any sourced from a local .snouty.toml file or the file specified by
--settings or via the SNOUTY_SETTINGS_PATH environment variable. However, snouty
login will save the specified configuration and credentials to the "global"
files in your home directory.

Examples:
  snouty login
  snouty login --tenant "mytenant" --repository "repository"
  snouty login --profile "profile"

Usage: snouty login [OPTIONS]

Options:
      --tenant <TENANT>
          

      --repository <REPOSITORY>
          

  -h, --help
          Print help (see a summary with '-h')

      --verbose
          Log API requests to stderr (authentication tokens redacted)

      --settings <SETTINGS>
          Path to the snouty settings file (default: ./.snouty.toml; overrides
          SNOUTY_SETTINGS_PATH)

      --profile <PROFILE>
          Settings profile to select (overrides ANTITHESIS_PROFILE)
